🔐 Secure Deployment Key & Secrets Configuration
 
📋 Overview — Recommended Approaches
 
Method Best For Security Complexity 
GitHub Repository Secrets ✅ Recommended CI/CD workflows ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐ Encrypted, never logged Low 
Environment Secrets (Env-specific) Different keys for Staging/Production ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐ Granular access control Medium 
Deploy Key (SSH) Server pulling code from repo ⭐⭐⭐⭐ Single-repo only Medium 
Encrypted .env file Server-side runtime secrets ⭐⭐⭐ Needs separate master key Medium 
External Secrets Manager Production, multi-environment ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐ Best practice High 
 
Golden Rule: Never commit secrets to Git. Only  .env.example  with placeholders goes into version control 
 
 
 
🔑 Option 1: GitHub Secrets for CI/CD (Recommended ⭐)
 
Step 1 — Add Secrets in GitHub
 
Go to: Repo → Settings → Secrets and variables → Actions → New repository secret
 
Secret Name Purpose Example Value 
 DEPLOY_SSH_KEY  Server SSH private key Full private key content 
 DEPLOY_HOST  Server IP/domain  192.168.1.100  /  deploy.myapp.com  
 DEPLOY_USER  SSH username  deploy  
 DEPLOY_PORT  SSH port  22  or custom 
 PROD_DATABASE_URL  Production DB connection  postgresql://user:pass@prod-db:5432/appdb  
 PROD_SECRET_KEY  JWT signing key  long-random-secure-string-32-chars-min  
 STAGING_*  Staging environment variants (separate values) 
 
Step 2 — Use in Workflow File
 
yaml  
name: Deploy to Production
on:
  push:
    tags: ['v*']

jobs:
  deploy:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    environment: production  # ← Use environment-level secrets too!
    steps:
      - name: SSH Deploy
        uses: appleboy/ssh-action@v1.0.3
        with:
          host: ${{ secrets.DEPLOY_HOST }}
          username: ${{ secrets.DEPLOY_USER }}
          key: ${{ secrets.DEPLOY_SSH_KEY }}
          port: ${{ secrets.DEPLOY_PORT }}
          script: |
            cd /var/www/app
            git pull origin main
            # Export secrets securely
            echo "DATABASE_URL=${{ secrets.PROD_DATABASE_URL }}" > .env
            echo "SECRET_KEY=${{ secrets.PROD_SECRET_KEY }}" >> .env
            docker compose up -d --build
 
 
✅ Pro Tip: Environment-Level Secrets
 
Create Environments (Settings → Environments) and store secrets there instead. This lets you:
 
- 🔐 Have different values for  staging  vs  production 
- ✅ Enable Required Reviewers — deployment waits for approval
- 📊 See deployment history per environment
 
 
 
🔑 Option 2: SSH Deploy Key (Server ↔ GitHub Access)
 
Use this when your server needs to pull code directly from GitHub 
 
Step 1 — Generate Key Pair on Your Server
 
bash  
# On your deployment server — use ed25519 (more secure than RSA)
ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -a 200 -C "deploy@fastapi-react" -f ~/.ssh/deploy_key -N ""
# -N "" = NO PASSPHRASE — safe for deploy keys only

# View public key — this goes to GitHub
cat ~/.ssh/deploy_key.pub
 
 
Step 2 — Add Public Key to GitHub
 
Go to: Repo → Settings → Deploy keys → Add deploy key 
 
- Title:  Production Server — deploy-key 
- Key: Paste the public key output
- ✅ Allow write access? — Only check if server needs to push. Leave unchecked for read-only
- Click Add key
 
Step 3 — Use It
 
bash  
# On your server, clone/pull using the deploy key
GIT_SSH_COMMAND="ssh -i ~/.ssh/deploy_key" git clone git@github.com:ZyntroAI/fastapi-python-boilerplate.git

# Or configure Git to always use this key for this repo
git config core.sshCommand "ssh -i ~/.ssh/deploy_key"
 
 
⚠️ Deploy Key Limitations
 
- ❌ One deploy key = one repository only
- ❌ Cannot access GitHub API — only Git clone/pull
- ⚠️ No expiry — rotate periodically as part of security practice 
 
 
 
🔑 Option 3: Encrypted .env File (Server Runtime Secrets)
 
Good for when you need secrets available on the server but don't want plaintext files
 
Create & Encrypt Locally
 
bash  
# On your dev machine — create .env.production with secrets
# Encrypt it with AES-256
openssl enc -aes-256-cbc -salt -in .env.production -out .env.production.enc -pass pass:YOUR_MASTER_PASSWORD
 
 
Decrypt During Deployment
 
bash  
# On server — decrypt (store MASTER_PASSWORD securely in CI secrets)
openssl enc -aes-256-cbc -d -in .env.production.enc -out .env -pass env:MASTER_PASSWORD
 
 
⚠️ Never commit  .env ! Only commit  .env.production.enc  — it's safe to track in Git
 
 
 
🛡️ Server-Side: Secure .env Management
 
Production Server Setup
 
bash  
# On your production server
sudo mkdir -p /opt/app/secrets
sudo chmod 700 /opt/app/secrets  # Only owner can read/write

# Store .env outside git repo
/opt/app/secrets/prod.env   # ← Permissions: 600 (read/write only for owner)
 
 
docker-compose.yml Reference
 
yaml  
services:
  backend:
    env_file:
      # Path on server — NEVER in repo
      - /opt/app/secrets/prod.env
    build: ./backend
 
 
Permissions Checklist
 
bash  
# ✅ Secure permissions — critical!
chmod 600 /opt/app/secrets/prod.env   # Only file owner can read
chown -R deploy:deploy /opt/app/secrets
 
 
 
 
🧪 Best Practices Checklist
 
✅ Do This
 
Use GitHub Secrets for all CI/CD credentials
Use Environment-level secrets for Staging vs Production separation
Generate ed25519 SSH keys instead of RSA
Rotate keys every 90 days — especially deploy keys without expiry 
Pre-commit hook: Use  gitleaks  or similar to block accidental secret commits
Frontend warning: NEVER put secrets in  VITE_*  vars — they get bundled and visible to anyone
Review access: Minimal SSH user permissions on deploy server — read-only where possible
 
❌ NEVER Do This
 
❌ Commit  .env  or any file containing secrets to Git
❌ Hardcode API keys/passwords in source code
❌ Use the same deploy key across multiple repos
❌ Store private SSH keys in plaintext anywhere in the repo
❌ Expose  .env  files via web server
 
 
 
🚀 Quick Recommendation — Full Stack
 
Layer Where to Store Secrets 
CI/CD Pipeline GitHub Repository + Environment Secrets 
Server ↔ GitHub Access SSH Deploy Key (public on GitHub, private only on server) 
Runtime Env Vars Encrypted  .env  file or server-side secrets directory, injected at runtime 
Frontend API URL OK in  .env  — not a secret, just don't hardcode production domains 
JWT_SECRET_KEY / DB_PASSWORD ⚠️ CRITICAL — only in GitHub Secrets + server .env, NEVER in repo 
 
 
 
